{"records":[{"id":"xz9t9gjtw6","title":"Incomplete immunity in a natural animal-microbiota interaction boosts pathogen virulence","description":"Data analysed in \"Incomplete immunity in a natural animal-microbiota interaction boosts pathogen virulence\". Used to generate main and supplemental figures. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-29T19:04:41.678Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xz9t9gjtw6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xz9t9gjtw6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kim","last_name":"Hoang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Evolutionary Biology","Genomics","Bacteria","Caenorhabditis elegans","Experimental Evolution","Pseudomonas aeruginosa"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"h35bk6ctjz","title":"LINF_330040400","description":"46 kD virulence factor\nLeishmania infantum (strain JPCM5)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-11-28T18:01:43.979Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/h35bk6ctjz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/h35bk6ctjz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jose M.","last_name":"Requena","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gene","Protein","Leishmania"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rvxntbtscw","title":"Virulence Factor Database for 16S metagenomics","description":"Virulence-relevant KOs Database allows the prediction of bacterial virulence factor genes from 16S-based metagenomics using Phylogenetic Investigation of Communities by Reconstruction of Unobserved States (PICRUSt) algorithm. After normalisation for 16S rDNA copy number and total species count, KEGG Orthologs (KO) abundances can be estimated using PICRUSt pre-computed files and virulence-specific genes parsed using VFDB_KO_unique file.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-07-31T08:20:28.283Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rvxntbtscw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rvxntbtscw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Celine","last_name":"Pattaroni","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Silas","last_name":"Kieser","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Benjamin","last_name":"Marsland","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Metagenomics","Bacterial Virulence","Database"],"journal_issn":["19313128"]},{"id":"gp6ktdfr2d","title":"Original data for Co-Immunoprecipitations from \"The Arabidopsis TIR-NBS-LRR CSA1 guards BAK1 BIR3 homeostasis and mediates pattern- and effector-induced plant immune responses\"","description":"Arabidopsis BAK1/SERK3, a co-receptor of leucine-rich repeat pattern recognition receptors (PRR), mediates pattern-triggered immunity (PTI). Genetic inactivation of BAK1 or BAK1-interacting receptors (BIR) causes cell death. We found that the TIR-NBS-LRR protein CONSTITUTIVE SHADE-AVOIDANCE 1 (CSA1) physically interacts with BIR3, but not with BAK1. Cell death in bak1-4 and bak1-4 bir3-2 mutants is dependent on CSA1 and on components of effector-triggered immunity-(ETI) pathways, including EDS1, PAD4, and the plant hormone, salicylic acid. Effector HopB1-mediated perturbation of BAK1 also results in CSA1-dependent cell death. Likewise, microbial pattern pg23-induced cell death, but not PTI responses, require CSA1. Thus, CSA1 integrates pattern- and effector-mediated cell death pathways downstream of BAK1. CSA1 guards BIR3 BAK1 homeostasis, and de-repression of CSA1 in the absence of intact BAK1 and BIR3 triggers ETI cell death. This suggests that PTI and ETI pathways are activated downstream of BAK1 for efficient plant immunity. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-11-24T07:16:30.789Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gp6ktdfr2d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gp6ktdfr2d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Birgit","last_name":"Kemmerling","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sarina","last_name":"Schulze","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Liping","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dagmar","last_name":"Kolb","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","grant_id":"SFB1101"}],"categories":["Lab Plant"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"j9nk8rhbry","title":"Destruction of self-derived PAMP via T3SS2 effector VopY to subvert PAMP-triggered immunity mediates Vibrio parahaemolyticus pathogenicity","description":"These data taken together demonstrate that VopY is a T3SS2 effector of Vibrio parahaemolyticus that confers both enterotoxicity and cytotoxicity. As a EAL-domain containing phosphodiesterase capable of hydrolyzing c-di-GMP, VopY effectively suppresses STING-dependent immune responses triggered by c-di-GMP, which is produced by bacteria and hijacked by the host as a signal during bacterial infection.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-18T08:16:18.233Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j9nk8rhbry","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j9nk8rhbry"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xuan","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lantian","last_name":"Zhou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chen","last_name":"Ye","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhenzhong","last_name":"Zha","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chuchu","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chao","last_name":"Feng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yue","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qian","last_name":"Jin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jianyi","last_name":"Pan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pathogenic Microbes","Host-Pathogen Interaction"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"drcj4xbmkk","title":"Gut microbiota-derived N-acetyl-D-glucosamine alleviates systemic inflammatory responses induced by acute Toxoplasma gondii infection","description":"Toxoplasma gondii, a widespread foodborne protozoan parasite, infects nearly all warm-blooded animals and often causes severe inflammatory tissue damage. Based on the interplay between gut microbiota and host immunity, this study explores the metabolic regulatory networks of the gut microbiome during T. gondii infection by using acute infection models with wild-type and low-virulence strains.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-19T14:55:16.224Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/drcj4xbmkk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/drcj4xbmkk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jing","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gut Microbiota"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5ynbh62n3f","title":"ARG, VFG, and PARB detection script","description":"This computational script is uploaded as supplementary material for article \"Characterization of antimicrobial resistance genes and virulence factor genes in an Arctic permafrost region revealed by metagenomics\"\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-12-09T10:08:20.982Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5ynbh62n3f","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5ynbh62n3f"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Heesoo","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Antimicrobial Resistance"],"journal_issn":["02697491"]},{"id":"jb2bdczkp5","title":"Near-Infrared-II Chemo-Optogenetics for Deep-Brain Stimulation","description":"We introduced a near-infrared II (NIR-II)-mediated optogenetic neuromodulation inspired by HaloTag self-labelling system (HaloNeu). An engineered temperature-sensitive ion channel HaloTag-TRPV1 was developed and stably targeted activation through NIR-II photothermal conversion nanoparticles functionalized with HaloTag chloroalkane ligands in vivo  by irreversible covalent bond formation. This allowed non-invasive, long-term stable and cell-type specific neuromodulation and behavioral manipulation by low and safe power of NIR-II illumination. Moreover, the HaloNeu approach enables precise activation of VTA-triggered neural circuits, offering effective and stable intervention on symptoms of Parkinson’s disease (PD).","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-02T09:52:50.5Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jb2bdczkp5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jb2bdczkp5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hongqiang","last_name":"Yin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Neuroscience","Neuromodulation","Optogenetics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3g4zwnvs5g","title":"SKSR1 identified as key virulence factor in Cryptosporidium by genetic crossing ","description":"Cryptosporidium parvum is a major cause of severe diarrhea. Although isolates of this zoonotic parasite exhibit significant differences in infectivity and virulence, the genetic determinants for these traits are not clear. In this study, we used classical genetics to cross two C. parvum isolates of different virulence and used bulked segregant analysis of whole-genome sequence data from the progeny to identify quantitative trait loci (QTL) associated with Cryptosporidium infectivity and virulence. Of the 26 genes in three QTL, two had loss-of-function mutations in the low-virulence isolates. Deletion of the SKSR1 gene or expression of the frame-shift mutant sequence reduced the pathogenicity of infection in vivo. SKSR1 is a polymorphic secretory protein expressed in small granules and secreted into the parasite-host interface. These results demonstrate that SKSR1 is an important virulence factor in Cryptosporidium, and suggest that this extended family may contribute to pathogenesis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-29T18:29:48.115Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3g4zwnvs5g","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3g4zwnvs5g"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Tianyi","last_name":"Hou","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetics","Cryptosporidium","Virulence Gene"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2d7yr52phg","title":"MATE-GUARDING BEHAVIOR IN ANURANS:  INTRA-SEXUAL SELECTION AND THE EVOLUTION OF PROLONGED AMPLEXUS IN THE HARLEQUIN TOAD ATELOPUS LAETISSIMUS.","description":"Mate guarding in anurans may consist of a prolonged amplexus or copulatory clasp by the male starting well prior to egg laying. In this study we combined field observations and experimental studies of the Santa Marta Harlequin Toad, Atelopus laetissimus, to elucidate whether the prolonged amplexus in A. laetissimus evolved as a mate-guarding strategy promoted by intense intra-sexual selection in males engaging in amplexus displacement attempts. First, we asked how the ability of a male to successfully defend its amplexus status against interlopers is related to body condition and 13 other morphological traits. Second, we tested whether the following characteristics were associated with mating success and mate guarding in A. laetissimus: clasp force, amplexus duration, and males’ reproductive effort. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-01-10T17:11:58.054Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2d7yr52phg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2d7yr52phg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Luis Alberto","last_name":"Rueda Solano","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Animal Behavior","Sexual Behavior","Biodiversity","Biologicals","Anura"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":121989,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":129,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":121989,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":121536,"restricted":60,"embargoed":393},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2044},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1460},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":553},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":441},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":418},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":379},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":379},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":340},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":273},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":246}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":119426},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1890},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":176},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":126},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":69},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":55},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":52},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":37},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":30},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b